{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1443789-disorient-charm","id":1443789,"title":"Disorient charm","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-04-13T12:54:33-07:00","id":1443789,"title":"Disorient charm","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u2022 Disorient: The spirit can alter landmarks and directions. The Storyteller rolls the spirit\u2019s Gnosis (difficulty 6 or the Gauntlet rating, whichever is higher). </div> </div> </div>How do you guys run this Wyld charm? If the spirit makes the gnosis roll, how does this affect the PCs?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26645-pwtucker","name":"pwtucker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26645_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-13T13:46:56-07:00","id":1443799,"title":"","content":"That's a good question. There are a lot of powers out there that initially seem easy to adjudicate, but in practice leads to a lot of questions. Here is what I would do.<br /> <br /> I believe the intent behind that Charm is to simulate the very frequent scenes in horror stories where the protagonist goes, &quot;Wait, isn't that...? We're going around in circles! How do we get out of here?&quot; It's a great Charm for a Will O' Wisp type spirit. This is what I would do:<br /> <br /> Roll the spirit's Gnosis, keep track of the # of successes. I would say the power works on both the Realm and Penumbra simultaneously so PCs can't defeat it by simply switching to the umbra, or asking their pack totem for help.<br /> <br /> If successful, the PCs essentially become lost for a scene, but don't know it. How long that lasts depends on the specifics. Smaller areas to be affected, like an enclosed cave system or large manor, should take less time before the PCs realize something is wrong than a larger area like outside wilderness with virgin forest for miles around and the PCs are looking to get out. So for whatever specific situation, I would determine the amount of time that it should take them to get out, and increase it enough that it make sense the PCs realize they are lost. (Depending on the situation, it might be 10 minutes, an hour, or several hours or any other time that makes sense to you).<br /> <br /> Then you either have the pack leader or pathfinder make something like a Wits + Survival roll so they can doubleback and get on the right track, or just handwave it as part of your description, and move on. Not everything needs a roll. Do whatever works best for your ST style and players. <br /> <br /> \u200bYou may want to include descriptions like they hear phantom noises or lights, or their friends calling them looking for them, and anything else that might confuse them. But be subtle about it, you don't want them simply ignoring it. It works best if they think that points the way out and can be justified as the reason why they're lost.<br /> <br /> Then after enough IC time has passed to realize they <i>still</i> can't find a way out, that's when you have your &quot;We're going around in circles!&quot; moment of clarity. I would keep the combined time spent in that total scene from the Gnosis roll to now to be relatively short in OOC real time. Players don't want their time wasted. A scene that lasts several hours IC may only need 30 seconds of real time as the ST narrates and explains things.<br /> <br /> At that point, let the PCs discuss. If they ask the right questions to you as ST, you should be able to drive them to the correct answer (someone has used magic to confuse you, you can't get out unless you figure out a way). Some PCs might not even need to ask questions, they'll figure it out immediately. Some PCs might not figure it out until you tell them. Use your judgment as to how much help they need. You may need to ask them to make a Perception + Enigmas or similar roll to realize the truth if they are still having trouble.<br /> <br /> Once they realize something is preventing them from leaving, then I'd have someone roll their Gnosis to break through the disorientation (if you don't want to use Gnosis, then roll something else, but I recommend the Enigmas ability as that tends to be the &quot;key&quot; ability for umbral conundrums and problem solving). If the players come up with the use of certain Gifts, pack totem powers, etc. to help them, then modify the difficulty in their favor. I would use similar rules to one Garou leading their pack to through the Gauntlet: let one PC roll on behalf of the entire pack as he leads them out. The PC will need at least as many successes as the Wyld spirit had in their initial roll, so it could be an extended challenge.<br /> <br /> If the PC fails to roll as many successes as he needs, announce that, state how much time has passed, and then allow a second roll. If it still isn't enough, tell them they spend more time, and allow a third roll. Repeat until necessary. Keep real time to a minimum.<br /> <br /> After the PC rolls enough successes, the Disorientation ends. You then either let them escape, or have the Wyld spirit appear in a last bid to stop them. You can than roleplay that scene however you think is appropriate. Maybe its combat. Maybe it's a social interaction and discussion scene. Maybe it's a riddle contest or other final challenge. Maybe it's an opportunity to exchange chiminage. Use whatever you think works best for the spirit, the PCs, and the scene.<br /> <br /> You can always add complications as needed. Perhaps you want the PCs to have an extended RP scene while they are lost. Perhaps you have some individual scenes you want to run as an encounter or event while they are lost (like someone else who is lost as well, but has been lost for a long time!). Maybe they come across more and more difficult encounters or situations, so that the PCs risk losing Health Levels, Traits, Willpower, or something. If so, I would make it so that the first encounters cost nothing, but if they need more and more time, they face increasingly worst odds and losses.<br /> <br /> Another complication might be if the PCs are constantly failing, you ask for Rage rolls to see if anyone frenzies and have the group deals with it. <br /> <br /> You may have other ideas of your own. Use whatever you think would keep the encounter fun.<br /> <br /> PCs should always be able to find a way out. It's just a matter of how much time and effort is wasted by them. Only NPCs might risk being lost forever, and eventually dying before ever finding the means out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-15T09:57:27-07:00","id":1444041,"title":"","content":"This is gold, thank you.<br /> <br /> I'm actually giving this charm to the Hive totem whose caern the PC's are going to attack tonight. They're so confident about being able to call the retreat at precisely the moment things look a little too dicey that I'm excited to pull the rug out from under their feet and make them sweat when they realize they may not be able to so neatly retreat after all...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26645-pwtucker","name":"pwtucker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26645_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}